.TH GOSH 1 "2026-11-16" "gosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gosh \- mosh's session, with forwards, copies, the relay over ssh and path-finding beside it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gosh
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR [\fIcommand\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gosh
logs in to \fIhost\fR by ssh, starts
.B gosh-server
there (or
.B mosh-server
where there is none), and becomes the client of a mosh session: a terminal that survives roaming
between networks, sleep, and lost connections, with local echo on slow links.
.PP
Beside the session it carries port forwards, a command's input and output, and file copies
(\fBgocp\fR(1)). When UDP cannot reach the server it carries the session over ssh, and goes back to
UDP when it can. From there it looks for a way straight through NATs, or through a meetup of your
own. Each works by IPv4 and IPv6, IPv6 first where both answer. You choose none of this.
.PP
\fBNot independently reviewed.\fR See https://wmacevoy.github.io/gosh/USING.html.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-L\fR [\fIbind\fB:\fR]\fIport\fB:\fIhost\fB:\fIhostport\fR, \fB\-\-tcp\-forward\fR=...
TCP connections to \fIport\fR here arrive at \fIhost\fB:\fIhostport\fR as seen from the server, as
\fBssh \-L\fR does. May be repeated. The listener binds loopback unless told another address.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR [\fIbind\fB:\fR]\fIport\fB:\fIhost\fB:\fIhostport\fR, \fB\-\-tcp\-remote\-forward\fR=...
The reverse: connections to \fIport\fR on the server arrive at \fIhost\fB:\fIhostport\fR as seen
from here.
.TP
\fB\-LU\fR ..., \fB\-RU\fR ..., \fB\-\-udp\-forward\fR, \fB\-\-udp\-remote\-forward\fR
The same for UDP datagrams, of any size up to 65,507 bytes.
.TP
\fB\-\-fragment\-parity\fR={\fBauto\fR|\fBalways\fR|\fBnever\fR}
Whether to add parity to forwarded datagrams too large to send whole (default: auto, by measured
loss).
.TP
.B \-N
No terminal: keep the session and its forwards open until interrupted. Says
\fBgosh: connected; forwarding.\fR on standard error once they can be used.
.TP
.B \-\-stdio
No terminal: run \fIcommand\fR on the server with its input and output here, as
\fBssh\fR \fIhost command\fR does; for example \fBrsync \-e 'gosh \-\-stdio'\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\fR={\fBauto\fR|\fBalways\fR|\fBnever\fR}
Carry the session over ssh when UDP cannot reach the server: when needed (default), from the start
and nothing by UDP, or never. ssh must be able to log in without asking.
.TP
\fB\-\-punch\fR={\fBauto\fR|\fBnever\fR}
On the relay, look for a way straight to the server through the NATs in between (default: auto).
.TP
\fB\-\-meetup\fR=[\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR
A meetup of your own (\fBgosh-server meetup\fR \fIport\fR on a machine ssh logs you in to), through
which the session goes by UDP when the two ends find no way to each other. Its ticket is fetched
over ssh.
.TP
\fB\-\-stun\fR=\fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR[,...]
Plain STUN servers to ask where each end appears to be; \fBnone\fR to ask none (the default: no
server on the Internet is asked anything unless you name it).
.TP
\fB\-4\fR, \fB\-6\fR, \fB\-\-family\fR=\fIfamily\fR
IPv4 only, IPv6 only, or one of auto, all, prefer-inet (the default: IPv4 first), prefer-inet6.
With more than one address, one that has not answered in 3 seconds gives way to the next.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIport\fR[\fB:\fIport2\fR], \fB\-\-port\fR=...
The server's UDP port or range.
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\fR=\fIcommand\fR
The ssh command (default: \fBssh\fR), for example \fB"ssh \-p 2222"\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-server\fR=\fIcommand\fR
The server to run on the host. By default gosh-server, from \fB~/.local/bin\fR first.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-predict\fR=..., \fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-no\-init\fR, \fB\-\-no\-ssh\-pty\fR, \fB\-\-local\fR, \fB\-\-bind\-server\fR=..., \fB\-\-experimental\-remote\-ip\fR=...
As mosh's; see \fBgosh \-\-help\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GOSH_RELAY_LOG
A file to which the relays say what they are doing: falling back, a way through found, a ticket
fetched, registered with a meetup.
.PP
The command sets \fBGOSH_RELAY\fR, \fBGOSH_RELAY_COMMAND\fR, \fBGOSH_PUNCH\fR, \fBGOSH_STUN\fR,
\fBGOSH_MEETUP\fR and \fBGOSH_MEETUP_TICKET_COMMAND\fR for the client from its options. mosh's own
variables (\fBMOSH_KEY\fR, \fBMOSH_PREDICTION_DISPLAY\fR, ...) are read as mosh reads them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the session ends normally; 1 when an option is refused, ssh fails, or no server answers.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgocp\fR(1), \fBmosh\fR(1), \fBssh\fR(1). https://wmacevoy.github.io/gosh/
